--TEST--
Core\Jwt::verifyIssued refuses `alg: none`, a token MAC'd under the key the issuer publishes and an algorithm that key does not carry, each with the one sentence a forgery gets — `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`, `rule:security/protocol-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, counted. The key is the issuer's, and every token below is one an
// attacker writes out of what the issuer published: `alg: none` says there is
// nothing to check, and `HS256` says the *public* half is a shared secret —
// which it is, to everybody who read the JWKS. The algorithm coming from the
// key is what makes each of these the same not-authentic as a truncated token,
// so the assertion is that four attacks produce one sentence rather than a
// verdict apiece, which a member with an arm per algorithm passes on no line.
Core\Crypto\KeyPair $issuer = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519);

var $segment = fn (string $text): string => Core\Encoding::toBase64Url(
    Core\Encoding::encodeText($text, Core\Charset::Utf8)
);

int $now = Core\Time::now()->toEpochSeconds();
string $claims = $segment(
    "{\"iss\":\"https://issuer.example\",\"aud\":\"novis-app\",\"exp\":"
        . (($now + 900) as string) . "}"
);

string $input = $segment("{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}") . "." . $claims;
string $honest = $input . "." . Core\Encoding::toBase64Url(
    Core\Crypto::sign(Core\Encoding::encodeText($input, Core\Charset::Utf8), $issuer)
);
string $signature = Core\Str::split($honest, ".")[2];

// The two shapes of the classic attack: `none` with nothing after the second
// dot, and `none` carrying the signature the issuer really wrote.
string $none = $segment("{\"alg\":\"none\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}");
string $stripped = $none . "." . $claims . ".";
string $borrowed = $none . "." . $claims . "." . $signature;

// The confusion. A verifier that reads `alg` to pick its arm HMACs whatever
// key it is holding, and the key it is holding here is the one the issuer
// publishes — so this token is one anybody who can fetch a JWKS can sign.
bytes $published = $issuer->publicKey()->write(Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Raw);
array<string> $mac = ["iss" => "https://issuer.example", "aud" => "novis-app"];
string $confused = Core\Jwt::sign($mac, 15m, $published);

// And an algorithm the key does not carry, under the signature it does: one
// kind names one algorithm, so `ES256` here is a header disagreeing with the
// key rather than a second thing worth trying.
string $es256 = $segment("{\"alg\":\"ES256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}") . "." . $claims . "." . $signature;

array<string> $attempts = [$stripped, $borrowed, $confused, $es256];
string $first = "";
int $refusals = 0;
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($attempts as string $attempt) {
    try {
        var $forged = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<tainted {iss: string, aud: string, exp: int}>(
            $attempt,
            $issuer->publicKey(),
            "https://issuer.example",
            "novis-app"
        );
        echo "a forgery verified as ", $forged->iss, "'s", "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
        $refusals = $refusals + 1;
        if ($first == "") {
            $first = $refused->message;
        }
        if ($refused->message == $first) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $refusals, " refusals, ", $agreed, " of them the same sentence", "\n";

// Nothing above consumed the token the issuer signed, so the four refusals are
// about the attempts and not about a member that had stopped reading tokens.
var $back = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<tainted {iss: string, aud: string, exp: int}>(
    $honest,
    $issuer->publicKey(),
    "https://issuer.example",
    "novis-app"
);
echo "the issuer's own token is still ", $back->aud, "'s", "\n";
echo $first, "\n";
--EXPECT--
4 refusals, 4 of them the same sentence
the issuer's own token is still novis-app's
Core\Jwt::verifyIssued(): $token is not a token this issuer's key signed. Every way of not being one — a shape that is not three base64url parts, a header carrying a parameter a verifier may not honour, a `kid` that names no key in the set, an `alg` that is not the one that key carries, a signature under another key, and an altered payload — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
